A||||16||Pheagey||Performance rotors and pads||||5||
Z||000000||Pheagey||10-05-2001||10:40 PM||||For those who know:<BR>Between the performace rotors from <A HREF="http://www.feirostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.feirostore.com</A>  and <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cwestco.com</A>  which is better? (Better as in stopping power, for shows, etc.) No matter which way I go I still plan on getting the Carbomet brake padds unless someone knopws of something  better.<BR>P.S.How can I get a pic to show up in my signature?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||10-05-2001||10:53 PM||||I'm looking at a Baer Brakes catalog and their exact wording on rotor treatments:<BR>"In testing, we have never witnessed a measureable difference between a slotted or crossdrilled rotor verses one without surface modification.... From an engineering standpoint, improvements from crossdrilling or slotting, other than cosmetic, can only occur after the brake pads have reached a temperature high enough to outgass, which is virtually never witnessed on the street."||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-05-2001||11:22 PM||||While my brake upgrade article is targeted at eliminating some of the   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">  about the Grand Am/Berreta/Blazer upgrades, it contains information relevant to all upgrades. Including links to Baer and Willwood, companies well known for their aftermarket brake packages.<P>I strongly recomend you do a search on those carbon metalic pads. Many carbon metalics perform very poorly when cold. Most brake systems are cold most of the time... Only in race applications can you keep them in the proper temperature range to work the way brakes are suposed to.<P>For all other applications, good pads from Raybestos, Ferodo/Wagner, and numerous other brands will work wonderfully.<P>Outside of SCCA racing etc, The number one problem in Fiero brakes isn't the OE rotor setup. It's the fact the OE setup is more often than not way out of adjustment in the rear calipers. The adjustment procedure in my cave article eliminates that issue. When I first developed and tested that procedure on my own car it took me nearly a week to get used to how well the car stopped. It literally went from,,, "Christ, am I going to <u>not</u> hit that" to "Oh gee, That Light is red, I can stop with no problem..." Before the adjustment my pedal was at or slightly below the resting height of the gas pedal. Now it's clearly higher than the gass pedal. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pheagey||10-06-2001||02:05 PM||||I thank you both for the input and information into the actual gain from 'performance' rotors. How about cosmetically? Which do you belive would work better for shows?||||reg||3
Z||000004||Raydar||10-06-2001||06:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>I strongly recomend you do a search on those carbon metalic pads. Many carbon metalics perform very poorly when cold. Most brake systems are cold most of the time... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I will second this.<BR>I put a set of Carbomet pads on my Formula. They were downright scary when they were cold. Hard as a rock. You absolutely <I>could not</I> lock the wheels. Couldn't even make the tires squeal. And this was with brand new rotors. After they were broke in, they were a little better, but still not great. I'd go with something else.<BR>Also wouldn't bother with drilled rotors. Useless on the street, and can crack at the drilled holes. A broken rotor would suck.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||10-06-2001||11:32 PM||||My experiences with pads.<BR>I used to get Raybestos PG Plus, and the have now priced themselves out of my wallet.  I've run the $10 an axle sets, and they worked fine but squealed.  I've had the Carbomets, and I was less than impressed with them also.  Noisy, dirty, and no better than OEM pads.  Not worth the extra cost, IMHO.  Bendix Surestops from Discount Auto are very quiet, and are only $20 an axle.  Some pad sets are already beveled and grooved, also.  Just gotta check the extra sets on the shelf.  They also come with the squeal eliminator shims.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wannabe||10-06-2001||11:38 PM||||Try KVR performance pads. They are priced about $30 per axle and provide a noticable improvement in bite and all around street performance, with some tolerance for track use. <A HREF="http://www.kvrperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.kvrperformance.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||DKOV||10-06-2001||11:52 PM||||I did the whole Fiero Store cross drilled rotor/Carbomet pad thing and sure it was a HUGE improvement on the stock setup but...<P>Now I have Willwood 13 inch cross drilled/vented rotors with 4 piston calipers at each corner.  TONS of stopping power and that VERY cool Gold Colored surfacing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>THESE are the "sweet deal"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
