Z||000011||DKOV||10-07-2001||11:16 AM||||Phil...<P>Under a month now, not long enough for that.  I haven't noticed much/any brake dust buildup at all.  They use a pretty hard pad though...  Good point though, something to think about.  Although, I think things will be just fine.  What with their reputation and performance and all.<P>------<P>Phaegey...<P>Do you all remember Ryane Motorsport?  Well, they are owned by Held Motorsports now.  Held is where I got my brakes and THESE are the brakes...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://heldmotorsports.com/images/13fbrk.jpg"> <P>The web address is <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>www.heldmotorsports.com</A> <P>As you can see, they come with a brand new perportioning valve, master cylider, e-brakes, four piston calipers, BIG HUGE rotors, adapter plates, steel lines and all bracketry, nuts and bolts.  Very comprehensive package... I am VERY impressed!<P>For the 13 inch brakes you MUST, ABSOLUTELY MUST have 17 inch rims or better.  If not, you will not have the clearance.  If you have 16 inch rims, you can squeeze their 12 inch brake kit in there just fine, but you loose the "cool factor" as the 12s are no where near as flashy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Anyway, give Dave a call and make sure you tell him SCOTT FROM VANCOUVER sent you!  I've got a ton of other things coming from him so any good words for me may just get my stuff here faster  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'll have pics up soon, gotta get film, take the pics, have them developed, get them back, scan them, post them...  I have got to get a Digital!<P>If you have any questions about my experience with them... just ask!<P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000012||Pheagey||10-07-2001||11:51 AM||||Sweeeeeaaattttt!!!!! Man what did those cost you? 7-8-9 hundred?!? Man those are kick @$$ !! To bad I only have like 225 for 2 rotor and pads.<BR>&lt;cheesy plug time :smile:&gt;<BR>act now and and receive a heart felt thank you AND credit for helping a fellow fiero man make his fiero phat. Makes checks payable to 'Pheagey's Fiero Fund' P.O. BOX........<BR>&lt;/end cheesy plug&gt;<BR>AS anyone else noticed how Fiero's and women are alike?  For example: small and sexy bodies, turn heads of everyone they pass, always taking every last cent you have, and can quickly become an obsetions..||||reg||11
Z||000013||Phil||10-07-2001||12:11 PM||||I know that the lack of dust/debris boots is one of the reasons that they're not DOT approved||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||10-07-2001||02:03 PM||||I don't recomend calipers w/o "dust" seals for most people. The lack of that seal presents multiple issues. Nearly all are bad things.<P>If you are going to run calipers w/o dust seals..... <P>I strongly recomend regular fluid changes. The dust boot also keeps water away from the main seal. A missing or damaged seal alows water against the main seal which is quickly absorbed into the fluid.<P>You also need to give them regular inpection to make sure they aren't developing corrosion that could hang a piston.<P>It is even more critical that all petrolium containing products are kept off this type caliper. Any oil the gets to the main caliper seal will damage it. <P>You also have to watch how you wash the car and what you wash with. Many car washing products contain chemicals that can attack the main seals. The "open" design of many aftermarket wheels makes it very easy to spray water and chemicals directly on the calipers.<P>Both Willwood and Baer offer calipers with dust seals. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||DKOV||10-08-2001||02:29 PM||||You guys got me going on this whole DOT/Dust Seal issue... worried even.  So I've been making some calls and doing some research.<P>Here's what I've found...<P>According to the DMV, brake components are NEVER DOT approved, with the exception of the Fluids and pads you can buy from the local auto mart.  Caliper and rotors are only DOT compliant when considered WITH the vehicle.<P>In other words, there are "specs" by which a Caliper and Rotor are measured in relation to the car or truck they are used on.  Providing they are within that spec, they are DOT approved.  Not individually, but AS A PACKAGE.  Car and brakes included together.<P>The second issue was the brake DUST COVERS...<P>What I have found there is that dust covers primarily function to keep dust, salt, oil and moisture out of the piston sleeve area because of corrosion.  The Wilwood components are made of Billet Aluminum and Stainless Steel to begin with.  NON-corrosive materials.  Secondly, the tolerances are build to withstand environmental bambardment.<P>The NORMAL use for these Calipers are MOST of the NASCAR and NASTRUCK teams, Dirt and Oval racing (outlaws, late modles) and Drag Racing (NHRA approved).  After 300 miles of HARD racing, a splash of water later, they are as good as new.<P>Wilwood engineering states that it is "recommended" that, for track use, replacing the O-rings when you replace the pads to keep the Calipers in perfect "brand new" condition.<P>Sounded like alot of work until I spoke with some street rod and late model racers that have been running the same "T" pad compound for MORE than a year with NO dust/debris issues and the original o-rings.<P>In addition, when it DOES become time to replace the o-rings, simply pushing the pistons out a bit with some compressed air, rolling the old o-ring off and a new on in its place and pushing the pision back in is the ENTIRE job!<P>All this with the Calipers IN PLACE!!!!<P>As for chemicals, cleaners and oils...  First of all, NO rubber compenents should be heavily exposed to these harsh chemicals, including dust covers.  That aside, Do to the stainless steel and Billet composition, wheel cleaners, oils and chemicals are not detrimental to the calipers in any way.  The o-rings are the only rubber that may get "wet" but a splash of water and no problems.<P>In fact, whatever you do to a regular set of Calipers, you can do to these Wilwood Calipers!<P>Oh... except STOP AS FAST  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
Z||000016||DKOV||10-08-2001||02:30 PM||||Pheagey...<P>How much?<P>Oh... just under $3000.00   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>DKOV -||||reg||1
